WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.360
The sight of a pointed dorsal fin peeking&nbsp;
out above the water immediately evokes our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.640
fight or flight response, but should sharks&nbsp;
really cause humans so much anxiety? Or did we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.400
sensationalize and demonize a fish into a monster?
Not to mention those people who have a general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.360
phobia of the ocean, thalassophobia.&nbsp;
The inability to see what is below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.560
a fear of drowning, of darkness, of the&nbsp;
unknown — these fears helped create a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.440
of sea monsters, both real and imagined.
Throw in a carnivorous fish and it's easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.480
to make a real animal a monster—and a lot of&nbsp;
people have capitalized on that with the shark.&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.080
Yet despite the rarity of shark attacks on&nbsp;
humans, these animals have an unwarranted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.600
reputation as blood-thirsty maneaters. And you&nbsp;
can largely thank horror movies for the bad PR.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.800
So if sharks are not naturally aggressive&nbsp;
towards humans, why did we turn a fish that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.560
kills fewer people a year than falling&nbsp;
coconuts and bee stings into such an iconic killer?

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.640
The idea of the shark as a monster is&nbsp;
certainly linked to their powerful jaws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.000
muscular bodies, and celebrated olfactory&nbsp;
abilities—not to mention those teeth.&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.680
I&nbsp;find a lot of sharks cute, but even I don’t&nbsp;
want to see those pearly whites up close.&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.520
They are powerful creatures that remind humans we&nbsp;
are not the animals at the top of the food chain.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.280
Perhaps fear of sharks is simply a&nbsp;
lingering biological response to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.160
possibility of being eaten&nbsp;
by these capable hunters.&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:41.040
Still, sharks don’t hunt and eat humans, unlike&nbsp;
some lions and other big cats, bears, or hell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.800
even pythons. Yet they frequently terrorize&nbsp;
us on the big screen and in newspapers alike.&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.480
There are a few shark-type monsters in mythology&nbsp;
and folklore, like the Japanese yokai Isonade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.520
the Classical Greek monster Scylla with its&nbsp;
many heads and three rows of shark-like teeth,

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.160
or the primordial sea monster Cipactli from&nbsp;
Mesoamerican mythology, but there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.240
far more shark deities — although some like the&nbsp;
Hawaiian Nanaue could fall into either category.
 &nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.560
The Polynesian peoples both feared&nbsp;
and revered sharks. In some stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.680
there are monstrous sharks that eat people, but&nbsp;
there are also stories of sharks possessed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.440
ancestral spirits rescuing humans. According to&nbsp;
other legends, some chiefs kept pet sharks who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.920
recognized members of the family.
In Africa along the west coast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.800
some cultures included shark meat as part of&nbsp;
their diet, while others saw them as sacred.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.240
Either way, they learned how to live alongside&nbsp;
the shark since respect was paramount.&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.080
We start to see sharks in a more&nbsp;
predominantly negative light from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.680
European sources around the 16th century.
In 1553 Spanish historian Francisco López&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.480
de Gómara recounted Spanish conquistador&nbsp;
Hernán Cortés’s conquest of Mexico.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.440
which included the conqueror's&nbsp;
interaction with sharks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.440
describing them as “insatiable”&nbsp;
and capable of swallowing anything.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.120
While further describing sharks, it is written&nbsp;
that they will “attack a cow or horse grazing or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:11.040
drinking at the river’s edge and will eat a man.”
Slave-trade sailors heard of the xoc from the Maya&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.760
of the Caribbean and turned the word&nbsp;
to ‘sharke’ around the 16th century.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.800
Written historical records from slave ships&nbsp;
mention sharks, along with other fish, swimming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.240
alongside the boats, eager to eat whatever&nbsp;
was thrown overboard—from excrement to people.&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.880
In the 18th century, abolitionists used&nbsp;
violent imagery of sharks eating Africans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.680
thrown overboard from slave ships to further&nbsp;
emphasize the horrors of the slave trade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.440
Artists immortalized these haunting images.
More visual depictions of shark encounters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.800
appeared after a cabin boy named Brook Watson fell&nbsp;
overboard and had his leg bitten off by a shark in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.240
Havana, Cuba. The incident became the inspiration&nbsp;
for a famous painting: Watson and the Shark.&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.600
Then in the mid-19th century,&nbsp;
human-shark encounters became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.240
a more popular fixture on the global news&nbsp;
circuit when the Birkenhead Steamer sank.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.360
Newspapers reported that hundreds of sharks&nbsp;
occupying the surrounding reefs laid “devastation”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.800
to the survivors. According to survivor Benjamin&nbsp;
Turner, who was a child at the time of the wreck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.560
“A shark would come up, seize them by the leg, and&nbsp;
drag them down.” These vivid testimonies, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.480
accompanying images, led to one of the first media&nbsp;
representations of a so-called “feeding frenzy.”&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.000
Still, sharks were considered relatively&nbsp;
harmless until the 20th century.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.560
A series of human-shark encounters&nbsp;
in New Jersey in 1916 led to major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.560
newspapers labeling the animals as so-called&nbsp;
“man-eating monsters,” causing a wave of panic.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.640
Around the same time on the other&nbsp;
side of the world in Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:47.360
surfing was gaining popularity. As more&nbsp;
humans entered into shark habitats, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.720
news began to pay attention to fatal encounters.
Public and political outcry led to the “meshing”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:58.480
of metropolitan beaches; nets were installed along&nbsp;
the coastline to catch sharks that got too close.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.200
In 1958, The Shark Attack File was founded&nbsp;
to document global shark encounters.&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:10.080
Overwhelmingly, however, sharks were still at the&nbsp;
periphery of consciousness for most. Until Jaws.&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.200
The famous film is based on a&nbsp;
1974 novel of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:22.000
inspired in part by those 1916 New Jersey events.&nbsp;
When the film adaptation hit theaters in 1975&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.520
it was accompanied by massive fanfare.&nbsp;
Jaws defined the summer blockbuster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.680
It helped launch Steven Spielberg’s career,&nbsp;
gave us one of the most iconic movie scores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.680
of all time, and made a whole lot of&nbsp;
people terrified to go into the water.&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:43.427
We can partially thank Universal Studios'&nbsp;marketing team for that. Inspiring fear seemed to be their driving strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.427 --> 00:05:48.015
"It is as if God created the devil . . ."

00:05:48.015 --> 00:05:51.017
 ". . . and gave him . . . Jaws."

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:01.040
Teasers of the film ran on TV (to the tune of&nbsp;
$700,000 dollars) and merchandise with the iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:06.320
film poster was created. The fact that people&nbsp;
didn’t have much knowledge about sharks made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:13.040
easy for the media to exploit fear of the unknown.
That fear taps into not just being prey but how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:19.840
you might become prey— in other words, the&nbsp;
jaws of Jaws is what really freaks us out.&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.800
Jaws also plays on fears (and guilt) of war,&nbsp;
comments on mass hysteria, and criticizes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.840
reckless scientist. Comparisons of the film’s&nbsp;
mayor to the disgraced President Richard Nixon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.200
indicate parallels between the shark-coverup&nbsp;
and Watergate. And then there’s the Freudian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.480
reading of a giant male predator engaging in a&nbsp;
little voyeurism before attacking a naked woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:49.360
but there’s also the feminist reading that sees&nbsp;
the shark as a metaphor of the monstrous feminine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.520
and/or the folkloric myth of vagina dentata.
Regardless of how you interpret the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:01.840
it's indisputable that the Great White Shark&nbsp;
at its center is meant to be the monster.&nbsp;

00:07:10.003 --> 00:07:12.005
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.640
A lot went wrong during the filming of Jaws,&nbsp;
including rough ocean waters, bad weather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:21.920
and the mechanical shark’s frequent malfunctions.&nbsp;
Nicknamed Bruce after Spielberg’s lawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:27.200
the animatronic shark used in the film was&nbsp;
notoriously finicky. The exposure to saltwater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:31.760
in Martha’s Vineyard led to the mechanic’s&nbsp;
erosion—and even greater unpredictability.&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.600
But these turned out to be what Bob Ross might&nbsp;
call “happy little accidents.” The inability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:43.600
to show the metal puppet on screen contributed&nbsp;
to its mysterious menace; we don’t see the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:48.800
shark until more than halfway through the movie.
In some ways, Jaws feels like an extension of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.920
the kaiju and “big bug” film genres, with&nbsp;
a little more horror and action thrown in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:08:00.000
alongside Hitchcockian thriller for good measure.&nbsp;
It’s man vs. nature with a slasher flick twist.&nbsp;

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:06.480
Post Jaws, sharks were handed the dubious title&nbsp;
of “monsters of the deep.” It probably didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.760
help that Jaws author Peter Benchley knew very&nbsp;
little about sharks. And most people took the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.840
film’s portrayal of a fictional shark monster as&nbsp;
the normal behavior of real Great White Sharks.&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:21.200
Immediately after the film’s&nbsp;
release, beachgoers headed inland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:27.440
damaging coastal town economies. Shark fishing&nbsp;
competitions became a thing. Despite the panic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:33.040
and the damage to sharks the movie caused, it also&nbsp;
inspired future scientists and the public to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:38.320
more about sharks in general. Benchley by the&nbsp;
way? He became a vocal shark conservationist.&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.840
Jaws solidified and popularized the&nbsp;
perspective of sharks as monsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:49.440
and ushered in the “sharksploitation” genre.
As digital special effects emerged in the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.400
monster shark films became even more&nbsp;
popular…we can’t seem to get enough.
 &nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.600
As special effects improved,&nbsp;
films began to make the shark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:04.400
even scarier—by making them bigger. Much bigger.
Enter the megalodon. The idea of the prehistoric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:09.680
fish surviving extinction was introduced in&nbsp;
the Jaws book, when Hooper, an oceanographer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.720
speculates that the titular massive white shark&nbsp;
is likely a smaller version of a creature lurking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:20.080
far deeper down in the ocean. He mentions&nbsp;
megalodons and questions if they are extinct.&nbsp;

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:25.360
In 1997, another author would pick up&nbsp;
on that thread and expand it—literally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:33.880
Meg: A Novel of Deep Terror by Steve Alten&nbsp;follows a scientist diving the Mariana Trench&nbsp;&nbsp;on behalf of the US Navy

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.720
who sees a megalodon and spends the rest&nbsp;
of his Captain Ahab-esq life trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:44.640
prove it exists. The book opens with an awesome&nbsp;
scene of a T-Rex getting eaten by a megalodon.&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:50.000
Unfortunately, the Meg books’ film adaptation&nbsp;
in 2018 didn’t include that scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.560
But it did give us Jason Statham underwater&nbsp;
fighting a fairly impressive CGI shark. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.720
While in the past the media&nbsp;
latched on to the sensational.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:03.840
With The Meg we got the requisite monster&nbsp;
shark attention but with a side of science.&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.032
"Mac, tracker is live!" 

00:10:05.032 --> 00:10:07.034
"Great, real him in, DJ."

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:17.840
The overwhelming majority of scientists&nbsp;
insist the megalodon is indeed extinct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:23.440
for millions of years, in fact, not hiding in&nbsp;
the modern depths of the Mariana Trench. 

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.960
Plus,&nbsp;the movie shark version is chunkier, longer, and&nbsp;
tolerates cold water better than the real thing.&nbsp;

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.920
As for its resemblance to a&nbsp;
great white shark?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:35.360
Evidence suggests megalodons were more&nbsp;
closely related to mako sharks.&nbsp;

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:40.560
These pop culture shark monsters still&nbsp;
have an impact on the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:46.000
Scholars and scientists have proven that&nbsp;
negative portrayals of sharks in the media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.520
fictional or otherwise, directly correlate&nbsp;
to how people conceptualize the real deal.&nbsp;

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:56.480
Even reputable networks like the Discovery&nbsp;
Channel capitalize on the shark-monster phenomenon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:02.240
with faux megalodon documentaries or “serial&nbsp;
killer” shark mysteries. These fictionalized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:08.720
shows rely not only on campy entertainment value&nbsp;
but on humanity’s appetite for positioning natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:15.360
species as supernatural monsters. The fish becomes&nbsp;
a serial killer, a spectral figure—a monster.&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:19.600
Many sharks today are facing the&nbsp;
threat of extinction—primarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:25.120
because of human actions. Threats to shark&nbsp;
populations include fishing, fin harvesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:31.600
pollution, habitat loss, and climate change.
It is estimated that 100 million sharks are killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.120
every year for commercial&nbsp;
profit. As apex predators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:40.000
the removal of sharks from the food chain has&nbsp;
a catastrophic effect on marine ecosystems.&nbsp;

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:45.680
Today, some scuba divers seek out shark&nbsp;
encounters, looking not for monsters but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:51.120
beauties, honoring the animals by respecting&nbsp;
them and studying them—not demonizing them.&nbsp;

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:55.840
Sharks aren’t devious, man-hunting&nbsp;
killers. We made them monsters.

